Here is the step-by-step guide to configure VPC peering between a public VPC (default VPC) and a private VPC, based on the setup in the video:

Step 1: Create the VPC Peering Connection
Open the AWS Management Console and navigate to the VPC Service.

In the left navigation menu, select Peering Connections.

Click Create Peering Connection.

Enter a name tag for the peering connection.

Under VPC ID (Requester), select the Default / Public VPC.

Under VPC ID (Acceptor), select the Private VPC.

Click Create Peering Connection.

Step 2: Accept the Peering Request
Select the newly created peering connection from the list.

Click the Actions dropdown menu at the top.

Click Accept Request to activate the connection.

Step 3: Update Route Tables
To allow traffic to flow between both VPCs, you must update the route table for each VPC:

Update Default / Public VPC Route Table:

Go to VPC > Route Tables.

Copy the CIDR block of the Private VPC.

Select the route table associated with the Default VPC.

Click Edit routes > Add route.

Set the Destination to the Private VPC CIDR block.

Set the Target to Peering Connection and select the connection created in Step 1.

Click Save changes.

Update Private VPC Route Table:

Copy the CIDR block of the Default VPC.

Select the route table associated with the Private VPC.

Click Edit routes > Add route.

Set the Destination to the Default VPC CIDR block.

Set the Target to Peering Connection and select the same peering connection.

Click Save changes.

Step 4: Configure Security Groups
Public EC2 Instance Security Group:

Go to EC2 > Security Groups.

Edit the inbound rules to allow SSH traffic (Port 22) so you can log into the public instance.

Private EC2 Instance Security Group:

Edit the inbound rules for the Private EC2 instance's security group.

Add a rule for All ICMP - IPv4 (or SSH if required).

Set the Source to the Default VPC CIDR block (allowing traffic originating only from the public VPC).

Click Save rules.

Step 5: Test Connectivity
SSH into the Public EC2 Instance from your local machine/AWS client.

From inside the Public EC2 instance terminal, run a ping command using the private IPv4 address of the Private EC2 instance:

Bash
ping <PRIVATE_EC2_IP_ADDRESS>
If packets transmit successfully, the VPC Peering Connection is fully configured and working.
